Reject a NaN or an Inf in the data of a state blob

The defects. tests/fuzz/core/run.sh test replays the reproducer of each one.

- A NaN in the data of a state blob gives NaN logits to the other sequences of the KV stream.

  llama_state_seq_set_data and llama_state_set_data copy the K, V and recurrent rows of a blob
  into the memory, and do not examine the values. A damaged blob that loads can thus put a NaN
  into the state of a sequence. A decode of that sequence then writes NaN K and V rows into its
  cells. The memory keeps these rows after a remove of the sequence, until a clear with data.

  The attention without flash attention reads each cell of its window: the cells of the other
  sequences and the free cells, with the weight 0 from the mask. But NaN + -inf and 0 * NaN are
  NaN. Thus each other sequence of the stream gets NaN logits, and each new sequence after the
  remove too. The flash attention of the CPU skips a masked cell, thus it does not show the defect.

  The change: the host reader and the file reader of a state blob examine the bytes of each tensor
  before they write them. A NaN or an Inf (for a quantized type, in a block scale) rejects the
  load, as a damaged size does: the load returns 0 and removes the sequence. The device reader
  copies the rows of a state that the same context wrote, and it has no host bytes to examine. A
  blob with finite values loads as before, and a decode does not change.

  The check of the types of the caches of the app (F32, F16, BF16, Q8_0) is one integer pass that
  the compiler vectorizes: the largest magnitude bits of the values (for Q8_0, of the f16 block
  scales) against the bits of Inf. It reads each value with memcpy, because the bytes of a blob
  have no alignment and the F32 and F16 loads of ggml_validate_row_data are then misaligned (UBSan
  reports them in the core suite). A different type goes through an aligned copy to
  ggml_validate_row_data. The per-element tests of that function in ggml-base run at 3.6 GB/s for
  F16 on x86, against 25 GB/s for memcpy.

  The host reader saves its reads and writes them later, as upstream does. Its flush() writes each
  saved read in parts of 256 KiB: it checks a part and then writes it while the part is in the
  cache, thus the load reads the blob from memory one time. llama_context::state_set_data and
  state_seq_set_data call flush() in their try block. A part with a NaN or an Inf stops the flush,
  and the load fails. The file reader checks the bytes that it reads before it writes them. On the
  box, one thread, the load of a 2B state of 4096 tokens (F16 KV, 67 MiB) takes 5.2 ms and a 4B
  state (178 MiB) 13.0 ms, against 4.2 ms and 11.3 ms with no check.

  The limit: a decode that overflows in one sequence can also write NaN rows, and this change does
  not cover that path. check_known_limit() in tests/fuzz/core/fuzz_recurrent.cpp tests the limit
  at the start of each run.

  The reproducers: tests/fuzz/core/regress/fuzz_recurrent--state-nan-kv-window (a new sequence
  after a remove) and tests/fuzz/core/regress/fuzz_recurrent--state-nan-kv-other-seq (a second
  sequence that stays).

- A failed sequence load kept a part of the sequence.

  The KV cache and the recurrent memory remove the cells of the sequence when their own read fails.
  But the hybrid memory reads the KV cache first: when the read of the recurrent memory fails, the
  KV cache keeps the cells that it loaded. A failure in the flush() above also comes after the two
  reads. Thus llama_memory_seq_pos_min showed a part of the sequence after
  llama_state_seq_set_data returned 0.

  The change: when a sequence load fails after the read of the memory starts, state_seq_set_data
  removes the sequence (llama_memory_seq_rm with -1, -1). A load that fails at its magic number
  does not change the memory. When the flush of a full state load fails, state_set_data clears the
  memory with its data, as the KV cache does when its read fails.

  The reproducers: tests/fuzz/core/regress/fuzz_recurrent--state-fail-kv-part (a damaged recurrent
  part), and property P6 of fuzz_recurrent with fuzz_recurrent--state-nan-kv-window (a NaN that the
  flush finds after it wrote the KV rows).
Base: llama.cpp commit c6824a9 with the patches of patches/series above this one.

